   "code_snippet": "if sys.platform == \"win32\":\n    p = Popen(command, universal_newlines=True, bufsize=0, shell=False, env=cmd_env)\n    _, _ = p.communicate()",
   "reasoning": "The run_command function executes user-supplied commands (from the commandline parameter of the CLI ‘run’ subcommand) without further sanitization. While shell=False avoids classic shell globbing, some forms of argument injection may still be possible if other components (e.g., the CLI upstream) allow manipulation. Dataflow shows untrusted user input from commandline in CLI surfaces flows directly to Popen (and os.execvpe) as argument vectors, without validation or whitelisting.",
   "evidence": {
    "dataflow_path": "User input from 'commandline' (cli.py:167) flows via 'run_command' to 'Popen(command, ...)' (cli.py:213). No input validation or restrictions are performed.",
    "code_snippet": "def run(ctx: click.Context, override: bool, commandline: List[str]) -> None:\n    ...\n    run_command(commandline, dotenv_as_dict)\n\ndef run_command(command: List[str], env: Dict[str, str]) -> None:\n    ...\n    if sys.platform == \"win32\":\n        p = Popen(command, universal_newlines=True, bufsize=0, shell=False, env=cmd_env)\n        _, _ = p.communicate()",
    "dataflow_trace": "See raw trace_dataflow_path output attached.",
    "additional_context": "While shell=False mitigates typical shell escapes, argument injection can still permit chaining, especially if command arguments are user-influenced. This is a recognized pattern in CLI runners."
   },
